## The idea

Read the string from left to right in the order its parts must appear: an optional sign, then digits with at most one dot, and at least one digit somewhere. Count digits instead of keeping complicated states. The full version (with an exponent) reuses this: split at the first "e" or "E"; the left part must be a valid decimal and the right part a signed integer.

## Step by step

1. IsDecimal: empty → false. Skip one leading "+" or "−".
2. For each remaining character: a digit → count it; a "." → allowed once; anything else → false.
3. Valid only if at least one digit was seen ("." and "+" alone are invalid).
4. IsNumber: find the first e/E. None → IsDecimal(s). Otherwise IsDecimal(left part) AND IsInteger(right part).
5. IsInteger: optional sign, then at least one digit, and only digits.

## Worked example

**Input:** IsNumber("53.5e93") and IsNumber("1e")

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| "53.5e93" | split at e → "53.5" and "93" |
| "53.5" | digits 5, 3, one dot, digit 5 → valid decimal |
| "93" | digits only → valid integer → true |
| "1e" | split → "1" and "" → the exponent is empty → false |

**Result:** "53.5e93" → true, "1e" → false

## Cost

O(n) time, O(1) extra memory (O(n) for the substrings in IsNumber).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// No regex and no double.Parse: those accept things like " 1", "NaN" or "Infinity".
public static class NumberValidator {

    // Optional sign, digits, at most one '.', at least one digit. No exponent, no spaces.
    public static bool IsDecimal(string s) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return false;
        int i = 0;
        if (s[0] == '+' || s[0] == '-') i = 1;          // one optional sign

        int digits = 0;
        bool seenDot = false;
        for (; i < s.Length; i++) {
            char c = s[i];
            if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {
                digits++;
            } else if (c == '.' && !seenDot) {
                seenDot = true;
            } else {
                return false;                           // a second dot, a letter, a second sign ...
            }
        }
        return digits > 0;                              // "." or "+" alone is not a number
    }

    // A decimal, optionally followed by e/E and a signed integer (LeetCode 65).
    public static bool IsNumber(string s) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return false;
        int e = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++) {
            if (s[i] == 'e' || s[i] == 'E') { e = i; break; }
        }
        if (e == -1) return IsDecimal(s);
        return IsDecimal(s.Substring(0, e)) && IsInteger(s.Substring(e + 1));
    }

    private static bool IsInteger(string s) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return false;
        int i = 0;
        if (s[0] == '+' || s[0] == '-') i = 1;
        if (i == s.Length) return false;                // just a sign
        for (; i < s.Length; i++) {
            if (s[i] < '0' || s[i] > '9') return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
